>> A2: In a few paragraphs, describe your code for locating the frame,
>> if any, that contains the data of a given page.

Each process maintains a per thread supplemental_page_table mapping page-aligned user virutal addresses to struct page
matadata entries. When a user memory access misses in the hardware page directory, pagedir, and triggers a page fault,
the fault address is rounded down to a page boundary and looked up in the current thread's SPT.

If no struct page entry exists, fault_load() checks is_stack_access(fault_addr, esp) to dertermine whether the fault is
a valid stack growth access, if so, it dynamically inserts a new PAGE_ZERO entry into the SPT. Once struct page is
retreived, we acquire p->lock and inspect p->frame. If p->frame is already non NULL, the page is already in physical
memory. Otherwise, page_load(p) allocates a physical frame with frame_alloc(p) and adds to kpage from one of three sources
depending on p's state: swapping back in via swap_in(p->swap_slot, kpage),if p->swap_slot != SWAP_NONE, reading
p->read_bytes from p->file at p->ofs and zeroing p->zero_bytes for PAGE_FILE / PAGE_MMAP, or zeroing the full page,
memset(kpage, 0, PGSIZE) for PAGE_ZERO. Finally, pagedir_set_page() installs the mapping in the hardware page table.

>> A3: How does your code coordinate accessed and dirty bits between
>> kernel and user virtual addresses that alias a single frame, or
>> alternatively how do you avoid the issue?

We check, and update accessed and dirty bits exclusively through the user virtual address v->upage in the owning process's
page directory, and by performing all kernel-side frame initialization before installing the user mapping.

Inside page_load(), the kernel reads file/swap data an zeroes trailing bytes using the kernel virutal address before calling
pagedir_set_page(p->owner->pagedir, p->upage, kpage, p->writable). If a page was loaded from swap, pagedir_set_dirty() is
explicitly called immediately after mapping so the kernel's swap-in write is never lost. During eviction in frame_evict(),
accessed and dirty bits are queried and cleared.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: When two user processes both need a new frame at the same time,
>> how are races avoided?

Frame allocation and eviction are synchronized using a two-level locking hierarchy combining frame_lock and per-page locks.

1. In frame_alloc(struct page *p), palloc_get_page(PAL_USER) is first called (which is internally synchronized by
palloc's pool lock). If a free page is returned, a struct frame is allocated, linked to p, and inserted into frames
while holding frame_lock.

2. If physical user memory is exhausted (kpage == NULL), frame_evict(p) acquires frame_lock to scan frames with the
clock hand. To avoid blocking or deadlocking with another thread currently faulting on or pinning a candidate page v,
frame_evict() uses lock_try_acquire(&v->lock) and skips any page whose lock is contended or whose v->pinned flag is true.

3. Once an unpinned, unaccessed victim v is selected, frame_evict() clears v->upage from v->owner->pagedir, reassigns
f->page = p, p->frame = f, and v->frame = NULL atomically under frame_lock, and then releases frame_lock before performing
slow swap or file write-back I/O. Thus, another process needing a frame can immediately run concurrently while the
first process completes its I/O under v->lock.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A5: Why did you choose the data structure(s) that you did for
>> representing virtual-to-physical mappings?
1. Hash Table for the Supplemental Page Table.
	A per-process hash table keyed by page-aligned upage provides O(1) average-case lookup during page faults and
	system call buffer pinning without allocating memory for unmapped regions of the 32-bit virtual address space.

2. Doubly-Linked List for the Frame Table.
	A global circular list (frames paired with a hand iterator) directly models the clock replacement algorithm,
	allowing O(1) insertion on frame allocation, O(1) removal in frame_free(), and sequential circular traversal
	during frame_evict()


